00:31:23.475 --> 00:31:27.340
kind of embarrassing moments of my life.

00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:32.248
And from there I really took it on to say,
hey, maybe I should take a little bit of

00:31:32.248 --> 00:31:36.690
the pressure off of myself and
approach it and have fun myself.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:42.332
I'm a clown and
it should be all about having fun, okay?

00:31:44.294 --> 00:31:49.214
Hopefully that works, okay,
we're going to try this again So

00:31:49.214 --> 00:31:55.180
I'm a clown and in my career,
it's all about how you make people laugh.

00:31:55.180 --> 00:31:59.277
And this one time, crickets, we went out.

00:31:59.277 --> 00:32:03.501
I was so nervous because I really wanted
to make sure people were having a good

00:32:03.501 --> 00:32:05.230
time at this event.

00:32:05.230 --> 00:32:09.633
Nothing, no laughter, not even a chuckle.

00:32:09.633 --> 00:32:14.880
Maybe the only laugh was an embarrassed
laugh and someone embarrassed for me.

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:19.820
I knew then and there that maybe I
had to rethink how I approach things.

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.980
I should be having fun as well.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:26.194
And so that was one of my biggest
learnings here from that failure,

00:32:26.194 --> 00:32:30.651
to have fun, because if I'm not
having fun, you're not having fun.

00:32:30.651 --> 00:32:36.070
Okay, two different ways of
answering the same question.

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:40.622
Maybe not as interesting as you
might have been expecting given that

00:32:40.622 --> 00:32:41.930
we're in an interview.

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:46.324
And you might be talking about
any professional experience.

00:32:46.324 --> 00:32:49.117
But in the first,
I just kind of started talking.

00:32:49.117 --> 00:32:53.275
I actually started rambling and found
myself going in a different direction.

00:32:53.275 --> 00:32:57.680
So I caught myself and
brought me back to the question at hand.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:00.240
The second, I mapped it out for you.

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:05.339
I told you what my job was, what I was
going to talk about what that failure was.

00:33:05.339 --> 00:33:10.503
I told you about the failure, and then I
told you the learning from the failure.

00:33:10.503 --> 00:33:15.790
If you map it out, it has this nice
effect of both keeping you inbounds.

00:33:15.790 --> 00:33:19.320
So having a nice starting point,
as well as an ending point.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:23.440
And that can be really helpful for
folks who tend to weave

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:28.095
very elaborate stories or
tend to continue beyond your point.

00:33:28.095 --> 00:33:33.345
And it helps your interviewer already
understand where this is going.

00:33:33.345 --> 00:33:35.957
On a map, you have a starting point and
an end point.

00:33:35.957 --> 00:33:38.935
Give them the lay of that land and
map it out for them so

00:33:38.935 --> 00:33:42.300
that they know which
parts to be looking for.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:45.066
In that first question,
you might have been waiting for, hey,

00:33:45.066 --> 00:33:46.570
what's the point here?

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:50.068
Whereas in the second one,
that point was a lot easier to pick up and

00:33:50.068 --> 00:33:52.540
also a lot easier to
communicate from my end.

00:33:52.540 --> 00:33:55.448
So give that a try.

00:33:55.448 --> 00:33:59.290
Okay, so this next one,
let's see, where did it go?

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:05.958
We're going to do the zookeeper one
time I had a difficult conversation with

00:34:05.958 --> 00:34:06.850
a coworker.

00:34:08.930 --> 00:34:12.530
And clearly, I'm a little bit here,
so I was going to choose that one.

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:15.440
I'm so happy it jumped into
the top five so I could.

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:16.590
Okay, here we go.

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:23.306
So this one time we were at the zoo,
where we were working,

00:34:23.306 --> 00:34:29.220
and we had this really strange
incident that kept happening.

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:33.316
We kept finding the antelope
with the lions.

00:34:33.316 --> 00:34:37.292
And every day, every night,
that was something that kept happening.

00:34:37.292 --> 00:34:38.905
We didn't know where it was coming from.

00:34:38.905 --> 00:34:41.405
We launched an investigation.

00:34:41.405 --> 00:34:43.909
Cameras were broken, so that didn't help.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:48.634
Finally, we figured out that
it was one of our coworkers.

00:34:48.634 --> 00:34:53.578
They thought it was really cute to
have a whole Safari in one space.

00:34:53.578 --> 00:34:55.607
And we had to talk to them and

00:34:55.607 --> 00:35:00.203
tell them that wasn't possible and
that we had to let them go.

00:35:00.203 --> 00:35:02.380
So I'm going to stop story there.

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:06.830
Next, let me try that again.

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.678
So I'm a zookeeper.

00:35:08.678 --> 00:35:14.265
And we had a difficult conversation
that I had to really take the lead on.

00:35:14.265 --> 00:35:18.797
Well, there was a period of time where we
kept finding the antelope with the lions,

00:35:18.797 --> 00:35:22.315
which didn't always turn out well for
the antelope.

00:35:22.315 --> 00:35:24.371
So I launched an investigation.

00:35:24.371 --> 00:35:28.710
I tried to think, what are the different
ways that this could happen?

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:31.850
I tried to look into the cameras'
situation, but those were broken.

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:37.012
And we realized after
talking to a number of folks,

00:35:37.012 --> 00:35:40.872
I was able to trace it back
to one of my coworkers.

00:35:40.872 --> 00:35:46.980
I talked to management and he said,
this is one of your direct supervisees.

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:49.700
You're going to need to tell
them we have to let them go.

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:55.104
So I had the really difficult situation of
telling someone who really loved their job

00:35:55.104 --> 00:36:00.140
and really wanted to create a fun, lively
[LAUGH] exhibit that they were fired.

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:06.458
Okay, hopefully those two
situations illustrated

00:36:06.458 --> 00:36:09.933
something that came out maybe clearly.

00:36:09.933 --> 00:36:13.090
As an interviewer,
I do start to pick it up.

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:17.097
There's a difference between we and
I, right?

00:36:17.097 --> 00:36:22.050
There is no I in team, but
there is an I in admit.

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:25.436
And I'm just kind of joking
with this kind of phrase,

00:36:25.436 --> 00:36:27.780
but there is some truth to it as well.

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:30.500
I'm guilty of this.

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.266
I talk in the we all the time.

00:36:33.266 --> 00:36:38.360
The last time I interviewed within
the office, Dean Moss was my interviewer.

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:42.143
And she said, Will, you keep saying we.

00:36:42.143 --> 00:36:43.070
What did you do?

00:36:44.330 --> 00:36:49.150
And so I encourage you as you're thinking
about these examples, what did you do?

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:52.517
Even if it was a group exercise,

00:36:52.517 --> 00:36:58.400
if it was a group that got from point
A to point B, you had some role in there.

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:00.290
What was your contribution?

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:02.140
Was it the idea?

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:05.860
Was it the late night that really
got you to that breakthrough?

00:37:05.860 --> 00:37:11.277
Was it engaging and getting the senior
administration on board with you?

00:37:11.277 --> 00:37:13.890
So it's okay to talk about I, right?

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:19.389
I know I mentioned in other sessions that
sometimes there is a cultural element.

00:37:19.389 --> 00:37:21.692
Maybe it's a community or family or

00:37:21.692 --> 00:37:27.270
personal kind of situation where you
don't really talk about yourself.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:32.670
This is one of those times that I
encourage you to embrace that discomfort.

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:36.175
And if you catch yourself going there,
it's always okay to name it.

00:37:36.175 --> 00:37:40.527
Name it to yourself because once you
name something, you're able to say, hey,

00:37:40.527 --> 00:37:43.155
okay, now we can work
towards addressing that.

00:37:43.155 --> 00:37:48.460
So take me there, walk me and
talk me through all the details.

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:51.851
STAR, situation, task, action, result.

00:37:51.851 --> 00:37:56.574
STAR+, give me some of that context as
you're planning or remembering things.

00:37:56.574 --> 00:37:59.720
What were the circumstances
that surrounded it?

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:01.090
What were some of those ripple effects?

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:03.660
Map it out.

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:06.780
Let me know as your interviewer
where you're going to go with.

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:09.263
Let me know so
that I can be paying attention and

00:38:09.263 --> 00:38:12.385
really focus on the details and
the competencies that come

00:38:12.385 --> 00:38:16.223
out rather than trying to figure out
where we're going with the story.

00:38:18.062 --> 00:38:19.197
Center yourself, and

00:38:19.197 --> 00:38:24.030
that is a phrase that I've been saying
pretty consistently through the week.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:28.912
Even though we do celebrate collaborative
effort, and the GSB is definitely one

00:38:28.912 --> 00:38:33.630
of those places where collaboration
is part of the experience, right?

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:38.130
How do you support others and challenge
each other to grow to the fullest extent?

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:42.003
I mean, the policy of grade
non-disclosure really exemplifies that,

00:38:42.003 --> 00:38:45.760
not publishing lists of GPA or
class rank to recruiters.

00:38:45.760 --> 00:38:49.636
This frees you to help each other,
to ask for help, and

00:38:49.636 --> 00:38:54.796
to create that support network so
you're always lifting each other up.

00:38:54.796 --> 00:38:59.650
And you're part of it and you should
be the center of this discussion.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:04.251
So don't worry about not feeling
like you're being a teammate by

00:39:04.251 --> 00:39:05.680
using the phrase I.

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:08.490
I really do want to know about
what you did and how you did it.

00:39:10.220 --> 00:39:15.353
So networking, interview,
two parts of the application process

00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:21.600
that are going to be really dependent and
really fueled by your voice.

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:23.231
So definitely practice.

00:39:23.231 --> 00:39:24.650
Find someone you trust.

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:28.913
Maybe this is an instance where you
practice on that networking site.

00:39:28.913 --> 00:39:31.509
Invite someone at work to a one-on-one so

00:39:31.509 --> 00:39:36.410
you can learn more about their kind
of job responsibilities and role.

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:41.594
Sometimes low-key interviewing like
that will help you as someone who's

00:39:41.594 --> 00:39:47.496
speaking on the other end feel comfortable
about, this is just a conversation.

00:39:47.496 --> 00:39:49.996
Talking to people really
does help with this.

00:39:49.996 --> 00:39:54.070
And I know that might be exhausting for
some, it might be energizing for others.

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:57.266
So find those times where you're
willing to exert that and

00:39:57.266 --> 00:40:01.193
find those times where you an honor
the times you just need to recharge.

00:40:03.590 --> 00:40:07.549
And this brings us here.

00:40:07.549 --> 00:40:12.115
When I started, in admissions, like I,

00:40:12.115 --> 00:40:15.249
there was a lot of learning.

00:40:15.249 --> 00:40:18.820
I realized that as I was growing up
I knew actually nothing about what it

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:21.313
meant to apply to college,
or even grad school.

00:40:21.313 --> 00:40:26.191
It often felt like looking back there
might have been some kind of hidden

00:40:26.191 --> 00:40:29.462
curriculum to, how to get in and
how to thrive.

00:40:29.462 --> 00:40:32.567
And so
my goal with the events this week and

00:40:32.567 --> 00:40:38.071
my goal with the sharing your story
series was to illuminate the process so

00:40:38.071 --> 00:40:44.311
that you can put your best foot forward so
that you can understand both our process.

00:40:44.311 --> 00:40:49.396
Just to be comfortable and know that
there is a system that cares takes this

00:40:49.396 --> 00:40:54.742
very seriously and is rooting for you and
know what's on your side of the net.

00:40:54.742 --> 00:40:58.268
Understand the things that
are under your control and

00:40:58.268 --> 00:41:02.888
the things that you are able to like
shape and shine and really mold into

00:41:02.888 --> 00:41:07.293
something that you are proud of
when you click that Submit button.

00:41:07.293 --> 00:41:12.209
The goal of application week was to help
you figure out the ways pick up some

00:41:12.209 --> 00:41:16.670
frameworks, some tools and
things that you can experiment with.

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:20.398
So that you can again,
put your best foot forward and

00:41:20.398 --> 00:41:23.124
see where this journey will take you.

00:41:23.124 --> 00:41:30.107
This slide is, a little anti climatic and
that's on purpose.

00:41:30.107 --> 00:41:34.978
This is just one step of your journey,
and maybe the least exciting step,

00:41:34.978 --> 00:41:37.347
probably the least exciting step.

00:41:37.347 --> 00:41:39.369
You have business school ahead of you.

00:41:39.369 --> 00:41:41.741
If you're not going to business school,

00:41:41.741 --> 00:41:46.496
maybe whatever career path you're going
on some of these things might be helpful.

00:41:46.496 --> 00:41:52.419
What matters exercise those five why's
can help you figure out, should I move?

00:41:52.419 --> 00:41:54.597
Should I take this job?

00:41:54.597 --> 00:41:58.463
What will that opportunity look like for
me, what is my north star?

00:41:58.463 --> 00:42:03.885
And so hopefully, this is again,
just the beginning of that crazy canvas,

00:42:03.885 --> 00:42:08.136
and that big picture that you're
going to be able to explore.

00:42:08.136 --> 00:42:11.927
And that horizon that you're
going to be able to reach towards.

00:42:11.927 --> 00:42:13.541
So I'm going to leave you all with this.

00:42:13.541 --> 00:42:19.009
It's an inscription on the cornerstone
of GSB campus and I'm going to just

00:42:19.009 --> 00:42:24.938
read it word for word because it is that
meaningful and that meaningful for me.

00:42:24.938 --> 00:42:28.995
Dedicated to the things that
haven't happened yet, and

00:42:28.995 --> 00:42:31.965
the people who are about to dream them up.

00:42:31.965 --> 00:42:34.093
Your dream big.

00:42:34.093 --> 00:42:39.198
All right, with that,
I'm going to take a moment.

00:42:39.198 --> 00:42:44.685
Go ahead and go into the Q&amp;A box,
answer whatever questions you might have.

00:42:44.685 --> 00:42:49.530
And I also really encourage you all
to tune in tomorrow to the dean's

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:51.917
office hours with Kirsten Moss.

00:42:51.917 --> 00:42:55.821
It's a fireside chat where
I'm going to be just.

00:42:55.821 --> 00:43:01.400
Shooting the breeze with the Dean of
MBA admissions and financial aid.

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:05.912
To really get her perspective
on this whole application during

00:43:05.912 --> 00:43:09.668
the journey that I hope that
you have enjoyed so far.

00:43:09.668 --> 00:43:13.018
And even if you decide,
maybe this is the end of my journey,

00:43:13.018 --> 00:43:15.097
that it was enjoyable and fruitful.

00:43:15.097 --> 00:43:17.067
So here's the question.

00:43:17.067 --> 00:43:19.319
Is networking with current
Stanford students and

00:43:19.319 --> 00:43:21.417
alumni important to
the admissions process?

00:43:21.417 --> 00:43:23.568
And how is that shown in the application?

00:43:23.568 --> 00:43:28.849
And so my favorite part of this
answer is no, you do not have to have

00:43:28.849 --> 00:43:34.624
interacted with any student or alum or
even attended a Stanford event.

00:43:34.624 --> 00:43:38.340
We do not practice what's
called demonstrated interest.

00:43:38.340 --> 00:43:40.420
Are you submitting your
application that's,

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:43.858
all you need to let us know that you're
interested in applying and attending.

00:43:43.858 --> 00:43:47.945
So please don't feel any pressure that
you have to reach out, talk to someone,

00:43:47.945 --> 00:43:51.727
especially if there's no one in your
network or you don't have the time or

00:43:51.727 --> 00:43:52.767
resources to do so.

00:43:52.767 --> 00:43:55.622
That is not a part of the process at all.

00:43:55.622 --> 00:43:59.389
We're here hosting events so hopefully
we can help you, kind of, understand and

00:43:59.389 --> 00:44:02.283
feel comfortable and
confident as you go through the journey,

00:44:02.283 --> 00:44:04.041
and that's all these events are for.

00:44:04.041 --> 00:44:08.894
So, no, you do not have to
interact with us at all [LAUGH]

00:44:08.894 --> 00:44:12.703
question about managing vulnerability.

00:44:12.703 --> 00:44:16.430
I want to talk about the past to take in,
and what our circumstances and

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:17.532
histories in play.

00:44:17.532 --> 00:44:20.206
How do we look at difficulties, setbacks,

00:44:20.206 --> 00:44:23.836
objectively negative experiences
such as work injuries?

00:44:23.836 --> 00:44:26.831
Some stories have uglier elements and
others but

00:44:26.831 --> 00:44:30.054
the learnings may be significant and
shareworthy.

00:44:30.054 --> 00:44:31.625
You caught it at the end and

00:44:31.625 --> 00:44:35.138
you said it I think way more
beautifully than I could have.

00:44:35.138 --> 00:44:39.234
The learnings may be significant and
shareworthy.

00:44:39.234 --> 00:44:43.254
If those learnings are,
then they are significant, right?

00:44:43.254 --> 00:44:45.582
Those are the things that
you want to anchor on.

00:44:45.582 --> 00:44:50.287
Let us know enough about the situation
that we understand what was going on.

00:44:50.287 --> 00:44:54.402
So if it was a work injury,
maybe that's where you can leave it.

00:44:54.402 --> 00:44:57.350
You don't need to go
into the grisly detail.

00:44:57.350 --> 00:45:00.867
So keep it at the level.

00:45:00.867 --> 00:45:05.040
Also read the room, I think that's
going to be one of those things

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:08.916
that you're also going to be
getting a lot of practice with.

00:45:08.916 --> 00:45:13.132
For some folks like with me,
you might get the sense that it's,

00:45:13.132 --> 00:45:15.097
you can go there and go ahead.

00:45:15.097 --> 00:45:17.529
And with others, you might feel like, hey,

00:45:17.529 --> 00:45:21.049
we don't have that relationship just yet
in this time and space.

00:45:21.049 --> 00:45:23.700
Let's keep it a little bit
more high level on detail.

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:25.764
But really going on the learnings.

00:45:25.764 --> 00:45:29.772
So it's also going to be a little bit of
dialogue between you and the person and

00:45:29.772 --> 00:45:30.800
you and yourself.

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:35.409
See how you're feeling in that moment.

00:45:35.409 --> 00:45:38.305
Let's see question about
about the demo [LAUGH].

00:45:38.305 --> 00:45:41.550
Hopefully it was more clear
than it sounded coming,

00:45:41.550 --> 00:45:43.992
then it sounded coming out of my mouth.

00:45:43.992 --> 00:45:48.052
For questions around failure or mistake,
is it better to include evidence of

00:45:48.052 --> 00:45:51.184
how you've improved in addition
to what you've learned?

00:45:51.184 --> 00:45:53.301
I would say sometimes yes.

00:45:53.301 --> 00:45:56.612
Sometimes though it's case dependent,
right?

00:45:56.612 --> 00:45:58.446
If that is the point of it.

00:45:58.446 --> 00:46:00.919
So maybe the question is, hey,

00:46:00.919 --> 00:46:05.235
can you tell me about a time
where you've had a failure?

00:46:05.235 --> 00:46:06.289
And what did you learn from it?

00:46:06.289 --> 00:46:09.869
Usually there's going to
be some cue there.

00:46:09.869 --> 00:46:14.626
That will let you know okay, should we be
talking about the learning or should we

00:46:14.626 --> 00:46:19.392
be talking about the growth sometimes
that learning and growth go hand in hand.

00:46:19.392 --> 00:46:23.693
If there's time, if there's space,
if you feel like you haven't gone on for

00:46:23.693 --> 00:46:26.963
too long, go ahead and
share those extra elements with us.

00:46:26.963 --> 00:46:30.948
What I will say is and this is where
he said it might be case dependent.

00:46:30.948 --> 00:46:34.597
Sometimes folks might go for
an extended period of time.

00:46:34.597 --> 00:46:39.858
I have definitely [LAUGH] had interviews
where I asked one question and these these

00:46:39.858 --> 00:46:45.202
are 45 minute interviews 45 to an hour,
and I only got through two questions.

00:46:45.202 --> 00:46:49.000
So also be conscious of the time
it should be a conversation.

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:52.792
And so, if you have a time,
take that pause.

00:46:52.792 --> 00:46:54.260
See where the conversations going.

00:46:54.260 --> 00:46:58.710
